<chapter xml:id="section-tangent-planes-differentials" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Tangent Planes and Differentials</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Derive the equation of the tangent plane to the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>
at a point <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> from the slopes
<m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> and <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m> of the two tangent lines in
the planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the normal vector <m>\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}</m> of the
tangent plane, where <m>w(x,y,z) = f(x,y) - z</m>, and write the
parametric equations of the normal line to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate the change <m>\Delta f</m> in the value of <m>f</m> produced by
moving a small distance <m>ds</m> away from <m>P_0</m> in the direction
of a unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m>, using the differential
<m>df = \left(\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \mathbf u\right) ds</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the linearization <m>L(x,y)</m> of a differentiable function at a
point and use the tangent plane as a linear approximation of the surface.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
Recall that the partial
derivative <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> is the slope of the tangent line to the
curve cut from the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> by the plane <m>y = y_0</m>,
and that <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m> is the slope of the tangent line to the
curve cut by the plane <m>x = x_0</m>. In this chapter we put these two
tangent lines together: the plane containing both of them is the tangent
plane to the surface at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m>. We find its
equation, use its normal vector to write the equation of the normal line
to the surface, estimate the change in <m>f</m> caused by moving a small
distance away from <m>P_0</m> in a given direction, and finally use the
tangent plane to linearize <m>f</m> near a point.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-tp-equation">
<title>Equation of the Tangent Plane</title>

<p>
We use what we learned about the partial derivatives and slopes of
tangent lines to calculate the equation of the tangent plane to the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m>.
</p>

<p>
Let us consider the equation of the tangent plane
<m>A(x - x_0) + B(y - y_0) + C(z - z_0) = 0</m>, where
<m>z_0 = f(x_0,y_0)</m>. We then rewrite the equation as
<md xml:id="eq-tp-plane-rewrite" number="yes">
z - z_0 = -\frac{A}{C}(x - x_0) - \frac{B}{C}(y - y_0).
</md>
</p>

<p>
When <m>y = y_0</m>, we have <m>z - z_0 = -\frac{A}{C}(x - x_0)</m>,
which is the equation of the tangent line at <m>P_0</m> in the plane
<m>y = y_0</m>, and therefore its slope <m>-\frac{A}{C}</m> is
<m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>. Similarly, when <m>x = x_0</m>, we have
<m>z - z_0 = -\frac{B}{C}(y - y_0)</m>, which is the equation of the
tangent line at <m>P_0</m> in the plane <m>x = x_0</m>, and therefore its
slope <m>-\frac{B}{C}</m> is <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m>.
<xref ref="fig-tp-plane-normal"/> shows the two tangent lines inside
the planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the equation of the <term>tangent plane to the surface</term>
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-tp-tangent-plane" number="yes">
f_x(x_0,y_0)(x - x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y - y_0) - (z - z_0) = 0.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that we can write the equation of the surface as
<m>w(x,y,z) = f(x,y) - z = 0</m> and define the gradient of <m>w</m> as
<md>
\nabla w = \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf i
+ \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\right)\mathbf j
+ \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf k.
</md>
Then, reading off the coefficients in
<xref ref="eq-tp-tangent-plane"/>, the <term>normal vector to the
tangent plane</term> is
<md xml:id="eq-tp-normal-vector" number="yes">
\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}
= f_x(x_0,y_0)\,\mathbf i + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,\mathbf j - \mathbf k.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-plane-normal">
<caption>
The two vertical planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m> cut the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> along the orange and black curves, whose tangent lines
at <m>P_0</m> have slopes <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> and <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m>.
The tangent plane at <m>P_0</m> contains both tangent lines, and its
normal vector is <m>\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}</m>. Equivalently,
<m>\mathbf n</m> is given (up to sign) by the cross product of the
direction vectors <m>\mathbf i + f_x(x_0,y_0)\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\mathbf j + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,\mathbf k</m> of the two tangent lines.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A gray dome-shaped surface, the graph of z equals f of x y, with the pink
tangent plane touching it
at the red point P 0. The orange curve cut by the plane y equals y 0 and
the black curve cut by the plane x equals x 0 run across the surface
through P 0. The blue tangent line to the orange curve at P 0 and the
dark red tangent line to the black curve at P 0
lie inside the tangent plane, and a magenta arrow,
the normal vector n, points away from the plane at P 0, perpendicular to
both tangent lines. A legend below the figure names each of these seven
elements.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\large]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{22},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=4.6,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none,
  clip=false,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,-0.04)}, anchor=north, font=\normalsize,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white,
    cells={anchor=west}, legend columns=2,
    row sep=1pt, column sep=8pt,
  },
]
% the surface z = f(x,y)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=21, forget plot]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% the tangent plane at P0 (pink)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-1:2, y domain=-1:2,
          fill=red!30, fill opacity=0.45, draw=red!50!black, draw opacity=0.6,
          forget plot]
  {2.825 - 0.25*(x-0.5) - 0.45*(y-0.5)};
