</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Everything in the figure above can also be explored interactively in
the <dataurl source="tangent-plane-explorer.html">Tangent Plane
Explorer</dataurl>, which opens in a new tab. It builds the picture up one
layer at a time <mdash/> the surface, the point <m>P_0</m>, the two
cutting planes and the curves they cut, the two tangent lines, the tangent
plane, and finally <m>\mathbf n</m> <mdash/> for any of several surfaces.
Drag <m>P_0</m> around and rotate the figure to see for yourself that the
tangent plane always contains both tangent lines and that <m>\mathbf n</m>
always stands at a right angle to it.
</p>

<p>
Also, note that <m>\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}</m> is the direction
vector of the line normal to the surface at
<m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m>, which means the parametric equations of
the <term>normal line to the surface</term> at <m>P_0</m> are
<md number="yes">
<mrow>x(t) \amp= x_0 + f_x(x_0,y_0)\,t</mrow>
<mrow>y(t) \amp= y_0 + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,t</mrow>
<mrow>z(t) \amp= f(x_0,y_0) - t</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp\in \R.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-tp-example-paraboloid">
<title>Tangent Plane and Normal Line to a Surface</title>

<p>
In the first example, we find the normal line and the tangent plane to a
paraboloid at a given point.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-tp-paraboloid">
<title>Tangent Plane and Normal Line to a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the surface <m>z = f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m> and the point
<m>P_0(1, 1, -2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the normal line to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-tp-paraboloid">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
<alert>A.</alert> We begin by computing the normal vector to the surface
at <m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>, which is given by
<m>\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}</m>, where
<m>w(x,y,z) = -x^2 - y^2 - z</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla w \amp= \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf i
+ \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\right)\mathbf j
+ \left(\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (-2x)\,\mathbf i + (-2y)\,\mathbf j + (-1)\,\mathbf k</mrow>
<mrow>\mathbf n \amp= \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}
= -2\,\mathbf i - 2\,\mathbf j - \mathbf k.</mrow>
</md>
Hence the parametric equations of the normal line are
<md number="yes">
<mrow>x(t) \amp= x_0 + f_x(x_0,y_0)\,t = 1 - 2t</mrow>
<mrow>y(t) \amp= y_0 + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,t = 1 - 2t</mrow>
<mrow>z(t) \amp= f(x_0,y_0) - t = -2 - t</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp\in \R.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
<alert>B.</alert> Note that we have already calculated the normal vector
<m>\mathbf n = \langle -2, -2, -1 \rangle</m> to the surface at
<m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>, and we are ready to write down the equation of the
tangent plane:
<md number="yes">
<mrow xml:id="eq-tp-ex1-plane">-2x - 2y - z \amp= D,</mrow>
<mrow number="no">\text{where}\quad D \amp= \mathbf n \cdot \vec{OP_0}
= \langle -2, -2, -1 \rangle \cdot \langle 1, 1, -2 \rangle = -2.</mrow>
</md>
Therefore the tangent plane is
<md xml:id="eq-tp-ex1-plane-final" number="yes">
-2x - 2y - z = -2.
</md>
<xref ref="fig-tp-example-paraboloid"/> shows the surface, the tangent
plane, and the normal line at <m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>.
</p>
</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-example-paraboloid">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m>, the tangent plane
<m>-2x - 2y - z = -2</m> (green), and the normal line
<m>x = 1-2t</m>, <m>y = 1-2t</m>, <m>z = -2-t</m> (black) at the point
<m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<description>
<p>
A downward-opening paraboloid drawn as a blue mesh, the graph of z equals
negative x squared minus y squared. A green tangent plane patch touches
the surface at the red point P 0 with coordinates 1, 1, negative 2, on
the side of the dome. A black line passes through P 0 perpendicular to
the plane: the normal line to the surface.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11.5cm, height=10cm,
  view={-40}{25},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.9, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.9, zmin=-8.3, zmax=2.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-2,0,2}, ytick={-2,0,2}, ztick={2,0,-2,-4,-6,-8},
  clip=false,
]
% the surface z = -x^2 - y^2 (blue mesh)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=blue!8, draw=blue!60,
          fill opacity=0.85, draw opacity=0.9,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=17, z buffer=sort]
  {-x^2 - y^2};
% the tangent plane -2x - 2y - z = -2 (green)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=0.2:1.8, y domain=0.2:1.8,
          fill=green!45!gray, fill opacity=0.50, draw=green!25!black,
          draw opacity=0.7]
  {2 - 2*x - 2*y};
% the normal line (1-2t, 1-2t, -2-t) (black)
\addplot3[very thick, black, samples=2, domain=-0.55:0.4]
  ({1-2*x},{1-2*x},{-2-x});
% the point P0
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=red]
  coordinates {(1,1,-2)};
\node[red, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.8,0.85,-3.9) {$P_0(1,1,-2)$};
% labels
\node[green!35!black, anchor=south] at (axis description cs:0.34,0.985)
  {Tangent Plane at $P_0(1,1,-2)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.34,0.985)
  -- (axis cs:0.4,1.6,-1.2);
\node[black, anchor=south] at (axis description cs:0.80,0.90)
  {Normal Line at $P_0(1,1,-2)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.80,0.90)
  -- (axis cs:2.1,2.1,-1.45);
\node[blue!60!black, anchor=east] at (axis description cs:0.09,0.72)
  {$z=-x^2-y^2$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.09,0.72)
  -- (axis cs:-1.6,1.0,-3.56);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="video-tp-normal-vector"/> works through this computation from
the start: it builds the gradient <m>\nabla w</m>, evaluates it at
<m>P_0</m> to get the normal vector, and then draws the normal line and
the tangent plane on the surface. Note that the video takes the normal
vector in the outward direction,
<m>\mathbf n = -\nabla w \big|_{P_0} = \langle 2, 2, 1\rangle</m>, which
is the opposite of the <m>\nabla w \big|_{P_0}</m> used above; either
choice gives the same normal line and the same tangent plane, since the
two vectors differ only by the factor <m>-1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="video-tp-normal-vector">
<caption>
Computing the normal vector for <xref ref="example-tp-paraboloid"/>. The
surface, the point <m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>, the gradient computation, the
normal vector, the normal line, and the tangent plane are built up in
turn, followed by a slow rotation of the finished figure.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-computing-normal-vector"
       source="videos/ComputingNormalVector.mp4"
       preview="default"
       width="90%"/>
</figure>

</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-tp-change">
<title>Estimating the Change in a Particular Direction</title>

<p>
Before turning to functions of two variables, it is worth recalling the
analogous idea from single-variable calculus. For a differentiable
function <m>y = f(x)</m>, moving a small distance <m>dx</m> away from a
point <m>x</m> changes the height of the curve by an amount that can be
estimated from the slope <m>f'(x)</m>: the resulting
<term>differential</term> is
<md xml:id="eq-tp-1d-df" number="yes">
df = f'(x)\,dx.
</md>
As <xref ref="fig-tp-linear-approx-1d"/> shows, this differential
closely approximates the actual change in the function,
<m>\Delta f = f(x+dx) - f(x)</m>, as long as <m>dx</m> is small.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-linear-approx-1d">
<caption>
The tangent line at <m>x</m> approximates <m>y = f(x)</m> near
<m>x + dx</m>. The differential <m>df = f'(x)\,dx</m>, found by walking
along the tangent line, closely approximates the actual change
<m>\Delta f = f(x+dx) - f(x)</m>, found by walking along the curve.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
The graph of a curve y equals f of x. At a point x on the curve, a
right triangle formed by the tangent line has horizontal leg d x and
vertical leg d f, equal to f prime of x times d x. A separate vertical
bracket to the left, labeled delta f, compares this to the actual rise
of the curve, f of x plus d x minus f of x, and a bracket to the right
shows the differential d f equals f prime of x times d x next to the
baseline height f of x.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tp-linear-approx-1d">
<diagram dimensions="(500,340)" margins="14">
<definition>curve(t) = (t, exp(0.35*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-0.5,6.2,4.9)">
<vector at="la-xaxis" tail="(-0.3,0)" v="(6.3,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="la-yaxis" tail="(0,-0.3)" v="(0,5.1)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="la-curve" function="curve" domain="(0,4.3)"
                   arrows="1" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<line at="la-guide1" p1="(2.5,0)" p2="(2.5,2.398875)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="la-guide2" p1="(2.5,2.398875)" p2="(0,2.398875)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="la-guide3" p1="(3.5,0)" p2="(3.5,3.404166)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="la-guide4" p1="(3.5,3.404166)" p2="(0,3.404166)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="la-tick1" p1="(3.5,2.398875)" p2="(5.6,2.398875)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.1" dash="1 2"/>
<line at="la-tick2" p1="(3.5,3.238482)" p2="(5.6,3.238482)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.1" dash="1 2"/>
<polygon at="la-triangle" points="((2.5,2.398875),(3.5,3.238482),(3.5,2.398875))"
         closed="yes" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5" fill="none" linejoin="round"/>
<vector at="la-dx" tail="(2.5,1.898875)" v="(1.0,0)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-dx-back" tail="(3.5,1.898875)" v="(-1.0,0)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-dfx-approx" tail="(4.6,2.398875)" v="(0,0.839607)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-dfx-approx-back" tail="(4.6,3.238482)" v="(0,-0.839607)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-dfx-actual" tail="(1.375,2.398875)" v="(0,1.005291)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-dfx-actual-back" tail="(1.375,3.404166)" v="(0,-1.005291)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-fx-base" tail="(4.6,2.398875)" v="(0,-2.398875)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="la-fx-base-back" tail="(4.6,0)" v="(0,2.398875)" arrow-width="6"
        stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<label at="la-xlabel" anchor="(6.0,-0.15)" alignment="n"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="la-ylabel" anchor="(-0.05,4.8)" alignment="w"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="la-curvelabel" anchor="(3.5,4.05)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=f(x)</m>
</label>
<label at="la-x-tick" anchor="(2.5,-0.1)" alignment="s"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="la-xdx-tick" anchor="(3.5,-0.1)" alignment="s">
<m>x+dx</m>
</label>
<label at="la-fx-tick" anchor="(-0.05,2.398875)" alignment="w"><m>f(x)</m></label>
<label at="la-fxdx-tick" anchor="(-0.05,3.404166)" alignment="w">
<m>f(x+dx)</m>
</label>
<label at="la-dx-label" anchor="(3.0,1.898875)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes">
<m>dx</m>
</label>
<label at="la-dfx-approx-label" anchor="(4.6,2.818679)" alignment="e" clear-background="yes">
<m>df=f'(x)\,dx</m>
</label>
<label at="la-dfx-actual-label" anchor="(1.2,2.90152)" alignment="w">
<m>\Delta f</m>
</label>
<label at="la-fx-base-label" anchor="(4.6,1.199438)" alignment="w" clear-background="yes">
<m>f(x)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The graph of a function y equals f of x, showing how the tangent line at x approximates the function near x plus d x.">
  <annotation ref="la-curve" text="The graph of the function y equals f of x."/>
  <annotation ref="la-triangle" text="A right triangle formed by the tangent line at x, with horizontal leg d x and vertical leg d f, equal to f prime of x times d x."/>
  <annotation ref="la-dfx-actual" text="The actual change in the function, delta f, equal to f of x plus d x minus f of x, measured on the curve."/>
  <annotation ref="la-dfx-approx" text="The differential predicted by the tangent line, d f equals f prime of x times d x."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The same question arises for a function of two variables. The
difference is that near a point in the plane there is no longer a
single direction to move in — moving away from a point requires both a
distance and a direction. Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y)</m>, a
point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in its domain, and a unit vector
<m>\mathbf u = u_1\,\mathbf i + u_2\,\mathbf j</m>. The question is:
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>
<em>How can we estimate the change in <m>f</m> (<m>\Delta f</m>) as a
result of moving a small distance (<m>ds</m>) away from <m>P_0</m> in the
direction <m>\mathbf u</m>?</em>
</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Moving away from <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> by the distance <m>ds</m> in the
direction <m>\mathbf u</m> is the displacement
<m>\mathbf u\,ds = u_1\,ds\,\mathbf i + u_2\,ds\,\mathbf j</m>, with
<m>|\mathbf u\,ds| = ds</m>, and it takes us to the point
<m>P(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds)</m>. As
<xref ref="fig-tp-change-schematic"/> shows, this causes the change
<md xml:id="eq-tp-delta-f" number="yes">
\Delta f = f(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds) - f(x_0, y_0)
</md>
in the value of the function.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-change-schematic">
<caption>
Moving away from <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> by the distance <m>ds</m> in the
direction <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2 \rangle</m> causes the change
<m>\Delta f = f(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds) - f(x_0,y_0)</m> in the
value of the function.
</caption>
<image width="95%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tp-change">
<diagram dimensions="(575,235)" margins="10">
<definition>beztop(t) = ((1-t)^2*1.5 + 2*t*(1-t)*4.4 + t^2*7.6, (1-t)^2*1.55 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.35 + t^2*1.18)</definition>
<definition>bezbot(t) = ((1-t)^2*0.7 + 2*t*(1-t)*3.6 + t^2*6.45, (1-t)^2*0.82 + 2*t*(1-t)*(-0.75) + t^2*0.78)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.9,-1.0,10.45,3.6)">
<vector at="cs-xaxis" v="(4.0,0)" tail="(-0.5,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="cs-yaxis" v="(0,3.0)" tail="(0,-0.5)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
