<m>f</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="On the left, the x y plane shows a point P 0 with coordinates x 0 y 0. A black arrow u d s leaves P 0 in the direction of the unit vector u, ending at the point P with coordinates x 0 plus u 1 d s, y 0 plus u 2 d s. Its horizontal component u 1 d s i is drawn in orange and its vertical component u 2 d s j in green. On the right, a horizontal z axis carries the two output values: f of x 0 y 0 in blue and f of the moved point in red. Two dashed gray arrows labeled f map P 0 and P to their output values, and the difference of the outputs is the change delta f.">
  <annotation ref="cs-P0" text="The starting point P 0 with coordinates x 0, y 0."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-uds" text="The displacement u d s: moving away from P 0 a small distance d s in the direction of the unit vector u."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-u1" text="The horizontal component of the displacement, u 1 d s times i, drawn in orange."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-u2" text="The vertical component of the displacement, u 2 d s times j, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-P" text="The point P reached after the displacement, with coordinates x 0 plus u 1 d s, y 0 plus u 2 d s."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-fbot" text="The function f maps the starting point P 0 to the output value f of x 0 y 0, marked in blue on the z axis."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-ftop" text="The function f maps the moved point P to the output value f of x 0 plus u 1 d s, y 0 plus u 2 d s, marked in red on the z axis."/>
  <annotation ref="cs-deltaf-label" text="The change in f, delta f, is the difference between the two output values on the z axis."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
We know from <xref ref="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/> that the rate of
change of <m>f</m> at a point <m>P_0</m> in the direction of
<m>\mathbf u</m> is given by the directional derivative
<md xml:id="eq-tp-dirderiv" number="yes">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\mathbf u, P_0}
= \nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \mathbf u.
</md>
Hence, we can estimate the change in <m>f</m> as
<md xml:id="eq-tp-df" number="yes">
\Delta f \approx df
= \left(\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \mathbf u\right) ds.
</md>
The quantity <m>df</m> is called the <term>differential</term> of
<m>f</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-tp-change">
<title>Estimating the Change in a Given Direction</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\mathbf u = \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\mathbf i +
\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\mathbf j</m>. Estimate the change in the value of
<m>f</m> as a result of moving away from <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the
direction of <m>\mathbf u</m> by <m>0.1</m> units.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-tp-change">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
Note that from the question we know <m>ds = 0.1</m> units. Our goal is to
estimate the change in <m>f</m>. We compute the gradient of <m>f</m> and
evaluate it at <m>P_0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= 2x\,\mathbf i + 2y\,\mathbf j</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla f \Big|_{(1,1)} \amp= 2\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf j.</mrow>
</md>
By <xref ref="eq-tp-df"/>,
<md number="yes">
<mrow number="no">df \amp= \left(\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \mathbf u\right) ds</mrow>
<mrow number="no">\amp= \left(\langle 2, 2 \rangle \cdot
\Bigl\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt 2} \Bigr\rangle\right)(0.1)</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-tp-ex2-df">\amp= 0.2\sqrt 2 \approx 0.28 \text{ units}.</mrow>
</md>
This means the change in <m>f</m> is approximately <m>0.28</m> units.
See <xref ref="fig-tp-example-change"/>.
</p>

<p>
For comparison, the exact change is
<md>
<mrow>\Delta f \amp= f\left(1 + \frac{0.1}{\sqrt 2},\,
1 + \frac{0.1}{\sqrt 2}\right) - f(1,1)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \left(3 + 0.2\sqrt 2 + 0.01\right) - 3
= 0.2\sqrt 2 + 0.01,</mrow>
</md>
so the estimate <m>df</m> is off by only <m>0.01</m> units. The right
panel of <xref ref="fig-tp-example-change"/> zooms in on the stretch just
past <m>P_0</m> and shows this gap between the tangent line and the
surface.
</p>
</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-example-change">
<caption>
<term>Left:</term> the graph of <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> and the vertical
plane <m>x - y = 0</m> that contains the direction <m>\mathbf u</m>.
Moving from <m>P_0(1,1,0)</m> by <m>ds = 0.1</m> along <m>\mathbf u</m>
moves the input point (light blue), and the value of <m>f</m> climbs
along the black intersection curve from <m>f(1,1) = 3</m> (green) to the
orange point. <term>Right:</term> the same curve plotted against the
distance <m>s</m> travelled along <m>\mathbf u</m>, together with its
tangent line at <m>P_0</m>. The window starts at <m>s = 0.05</m>, past
the stretch where the two are indistinguishable, so that the gap between
them can be seen: at <m>s = ds = 0.1</m> the tangent line has risen by
<m>df \approx 0.283</m> to <m>3.283</m>, while the curve itself has risen
by <m>\Delta f \approx 0.293</m> to <m>3.293</m>.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
