</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-tp-linearization">
<title>Linearizing <m>z = f(x,y)</m></title>

<p>
We can estimate the behavior of a function of two variables
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> around a point <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> by
making the observation that the tangent plane to the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m> can be used as such an approximation.
Below is the formal definition.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-tp-linearization">
<statement>
<p>
The <term>linearization</term> of a differentiable function
<m>f(x,y)</m> at a point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> is the function
<md xml:id="eq-tp-linearization" number="yes">
L(x,y) = f_x(x_0,y_0)(x - x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y - y_0) + f(x_0,y_0).
</md>
The linearized function <m>L(x,y)</m> can be used as an approximation for
<m>f(x,y)</m> near <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Note that solving <xref ref="eq-tp-tangent-plane"/> for <m>z</m> gives
exactly <m>z = L(x,y)</m>: the graph of the linearization is the tangent
plane to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-tp-linearization">
<title>Linear Approximation of a Function</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x</m>. Approximate
<m>f(x,y)</m> near the point
<m>P_0\left(-0.5,\, -0.5,\, f(-0.5,-0.5)\right)</m> with a linear
function <m>L(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-tp-linearization">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
We use the equation of the tangent plane at
<m>P_0\left(-0.5, -0.5, f(-0.5,-0.5)\right)</m> as a linear approximation
for the surface, as follows. First we evaluate <m>f</m> and its partial
derivatives at the point:
<md>
<mrow>f(-0.5,-0.5) \amp= \frac{3}{8}</mrow>
<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp= -4x - 2y^3 - 2
\;\Rightarrow\;
f_x(-0.5,-0.5) = 2 + \frac{1}{4} - 2 = \frac{1}{4}</mrow>
<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp= -6xy^2
\;\Rightarrow\;
f_y(-0.5,-0.5) = \frac{3}{4}.</mrow>
</md>
Then, by <xref ref="eq-tp-linearization"/>,
<md number="yes">
<mrow number="no">f(x,y) \amp\approx L(x,y)</mrow>
<mrow number="no">\amp= f_x(x_0,y_0)(x - x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y - y_0)
+ f(x_0,y_0)</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-tp-ex3-L">\amp= \frac{1}{4}\left(x + \frac{1}{2}\right)
+ \frac{3}{4}\left(y + \frac{1}{2}\right) + \frac{3}{8}.</mrow>
</md>
<xref ref="fig-tp-example-linearization"/> shows the graph of <m>f</m>
together with the graph of <m>L</m>, which is the tangent plane patch at
<m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-tp-example-linearization">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x</m> and the tangent plane at
<m>P_0\left(-\frac12, -\frac12, \frac38\right)</m>, whose equation is
<m>z = L(x,y) = \frac14\left(x+\frac12\right) +
\frac34\left(y+\frac12\right) + \frac38</m>. The view is close in on
<m>P_0</m>, where the plane hugs the surface, so that <m>L(x,y)</m>
approximates <m>f(x,y)</m>; farther out the surface curves away from the
plane. The arrow at <m>P_0</m> is the normal direction to the surface.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A blue mesh surface, the graph of z equals negative 2 x squared minus 2 x
y cubed minus 2 x, seen close up around the point P 0. A flat dark red
patch of the tangent plane z equals L of x y cuts through the mesh,
touching it at the green point P 0 with coordinates negative one half,
negative one half, three eighths: near P 0 the patch and the surface are
indistinguishable, while away from P 0 the surface curves down and away
from the flat patch. A long black arrow leaves P 0 at right angles to the
patch, showing the normal direction to the surface there. A legend below
the picture names the surface, the tangent plane, the point P 0, and the
normal direction.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\large]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10.26cm, height=10.01cm,
  view={-125}{25},
  xmin=-0.96, xmax=-0.04, ymin=-0.96, ymax=-0.04, zmin=-0.46, zmax=0.66,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-0.8,-0.6,-0.4,-0.2}, ytick={-0.8,-0.6,-0.4,-0.2},
  ztick={-0.4,-0.2,0,0.2,0.4,0.6},
  clip=false,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,-0.08)}, anchor=north, font=\normalsize,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white,
    cells={anchor=west}, legend columns=2,
    row sep=1pt, column sep=8pt,
  },
]
% the surface z = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x (blue mesh)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=blue!8, draw=blue!60,
          fill opacity=0.85, draw opacity=0.9,
          domain=-0.8:-0.2, y domain=-0.8:-0.2, samples=19,
          z buffer=sort, forget plot]
  {-2*x^2 - 2*x*y^3 - 2*x};
% the tangent plane patch z = L(x,y) at P0 (dark red)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-0.72:-0.28,
          y domain=-0.72:-0.28,
          fill=red!50!black, fill opacity=0.55, draw=red!30!black,
          forget plot]
  {0.25*(x+0.5) + 0.75*(y+0.5) + 0.375};
% the normal direction at P0
\draw[-stealth, black, line width=1.1pt]
  (axis cs:-0.5,-0.5,0.375) -- (axis cs:-0.567,-0.702,0.644);
% the point P0
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=green!60!black,
          forget plot]
  coordinates {(-0.5,-0.5,0.375)};
% legend
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=blue!8, draw=blue!60}
\addlegendentry{$z=-2x^2-2xy^3-2x$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=red!50!black, fill opacity=0.55,
                draw=red!30!black}
\addlegendentry{tangent plane $z=L(x,y)$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=green!60!black}
\addlegendentry{$P_0\left(-\frac12,-\frac12,\frac38\right)$}
\addlegendimage{-stealth, black, line width=1.1pt}
\addlegendentry{normal direction at $P_0$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</section>

